      "writtenOn": "2022-04-07T15:33:03Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Is it a problem if this close() call wins the race against the \"mPacketReader.stop()\" lambda just posted above? If so, you might want to run both the stop and the close from the lambda.\n\nAlternatively, does PacketReader stop cleanly when its fd is closed? I think it might because FdEventsReader#handleInput will log but not throw if read throws an exception. I think read is guaranteed to throw an exception if the fd is closed while it\u0027s running; this is not how linux behaves but Java/Android require it. See AsynchronousCloseMonitor and its users. Not sure if AsynchronousCloseMonitor will cause poll to fail though.\n\nAll that said - it seems simplest here to do teardown just by using mPacketReader.stop(), since I think that will close the fd. So maybe something like this?\n\n    if (mPacketReader !\u003d null) {\n        mHandler.post(() -\u003e mPacketReader.stop());  // Also closes the fd.\n    } else if (mTapInterface !\u003d null) {\n        mTapInterface.getFileDescriptor().close();\n    }\n\nThis seems way too difficult to get right. What do other tests do?",
